    (0, None, '\'Viktor, good evening. Where is Alisa?\' "Dobry vecher" is "kind evening", the sibling of "dobry den". And "gde Alisa" has no "is", as usual. Tanya is polite, and already suspicious.'),
    (1, None, '\'And she and I decided to stay.\' Russian says "my s ney", "we with her", for "she and I". "Reshili ostatsya" is "decided to stay". Barinov invents a plan on the spot, and he sounds very sure, which is how you can tell.'),
